          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place the cabbage pieces in a large bowl. Sprinkle generously with fine sea or kosher flakes (about 2 tbsp). Toss well and leave for 1 hour, tossing every 20 minutes, until the cabbage has wilted and released liquid."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Rinse the cabbage under cold water two or three times to remove excess brine. Drain in a colander and squeeze out as much water as possible. Pat dry with a clean cloth."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the grated garlic, ginger, "
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "gochujang ",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                        "attrs": {
                          "termSlug": "gochujang"
                        }
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", chilli flakes, fish sauce, and sugar together in a small bowl to form the paste."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Combine the drained cabbage, spring onion, and carrot in a large bowl. Add the paste and mix with your hands (wear gloves to protect from chilli staining) until everything is evenly coated."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pack tightly into a clean glass jar, pressing down so the vegetables are submerged under their own liquid. Leave 3 cm of space at the top. Seal and leave at room temperature for 24 to 48 hours."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Once it tastes pleasantly sour, transfer to the fridge. It is ready to eat straight away or after a further week of cold fermentation for a more developed flavour."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Kimchi is not sour after 48 hours",
              "fix": "The room may be too cold. Move the jar somewhere warmer, around 20 to 22C, and check again after another 24 hours."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Kimchi is too spicy",
              "fix": "Reduce the chilli flakes to 1 tablespoon next time. You can also rinse the finished kimchi briefly before serving to mellow the heat."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Cabbage is still tough after salting",
              "fix": "Salt it for longer, up to 2 hours. The cabbage needs to wilt fully before rinsing."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Kimchi is eaten at every Korean meal, from breakfast to dinner. It appears on tables alongside soups, rice, and grilled meats. Older, more sour kimchi is used in cooking rather than eaten raw. It goes into stews, fried rice, and pancakes. Every region and family has a slightly different recipe."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}